# Project skill settings

`project_skill_settings` once had 110 columns because every new AI workflow added its own skill path, model, and effort columns. Those columns are gone. Workflow values live in `project_skill_workflow_settings`, and the parent table stays at 33 columns. Postgres rejects a function call with more than 100 arguments, which is what made the old shape fail.

## Adding a workflow

- Never add a `*_skill_path`, `*_model`, `*_effort`, `*_bedrock_*`, or `*_score_threshold` column to `project_skill_settings`.
- Add the workflow as one entry in `PROJECT_SKILL_WORKFLOWS` (`src/shared/constants/projectSkillWorkflows.ts`). Its values live in `project_skill_workflow_settings`, one row per `(settings_id, workflow)`. The `legacy` map names the flat API fields, not database columns.
- A setting that applies to the whole project, not to one workflow, goes on the parent row only if the table stays under its ceiling. Otherwise it goes in a separate table.

`src/server/__tests__/schemaTableWidth.test.ts` fails if the table grows past 33 columns or gains a per-workflow column. Do not raise those ceilings.

## Reading settings

- Read through `projectSettingsService.ts`. It returns the flat `ProjectSkillConfig` built from the parent row and the workflow rows.
- When a relational query includes `skillSettings`, select only the columns the code uses:

```typescript
// ✅ GOOD
with: { skillSettings: { columns: { skillRepo: true, skillBranch: true } } }

// ❌ BAD: passes every column to json_build_array
with: { skillSettings: true }
```
